  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 155 AM EDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\n             -----------Synoptic Overview-----------\n\nThe Florida Peninsula lies between Hurricane Isaias in the Gulf\nand an H5 anticyclone east of the Bahamas this morning. Southerly\nmid-level flow is increasing between these two features. The\nquasi-stationary front that has sat over the state for several\ndays is beginning to migrate northward in tandem with Isaias. By\ntonight, the front should lift north of I-10 as Isaias makes\nlandfall somewhere near Pensacola or Mobile.\n\nSouth of the front and across Central Florida, anomalous moisture\nis expected to remain in place as southeasterly to southerly\nflow is enhanced. PWATs from 1.8 to 2.5\" are forecast through\nSunday. H85 temperatures also remain above normal, between +18 \nand +19 deg C.\n\nIsaias will become an extratropical low over the Tennessee\nValley this weekend before drifting into the Mid-Atlantic by early\nMonday. As the mid-level ridge retrogrades over the Gulf, drier air\nis forecast to settle toward Florida beginning on Monday. Later next\nweek, global ensembles shift the mid-level ridge axis back toward\nthe state as a trough ejects from the Western U.S. Total moisture\nshould slowly rise to near mid-October norms by the end of next\nweek but remain much lower than what has been in place recently.\n\n             -------Sensible Weather & Impacts-------\n\nThrough Tonight...\n\nSome modest changes are coming to our weather pattern today. As\nmentioned in the overview, the stubborn front that has focused\nrounds of showers and storms will finally lift north of the\narea. Moisture values also drop off just a bit but remain above\nnormal for the time of year. This will keep at least scattered\nshowers and storms in the forecast (50-60%). However, the loss of\ndeep convergence associated with the front should temper overall\ncoverage compared to yesterday.\n\nREFS members tend to have the greatest coverage of showers and\nstorms this afternoon and evening. With moderate instability and\nsomewhat stronger mid-level flow, a few stronger storms may be\ncapable of producing wind gusts of 40-50 mph. Storm motion looks\nquicker, which should limit the excessive rain threat. Any localized\nflooding would be limited to areas that see repeated storms in a\nshort timeframe.\n\nOne thing that will be noticeable: it's going to stay very\nhumid. With more sunshine on the way today, the heat and humidity\nwill make their presence felt. Prepare for peak heat indices from\nthe upper 90s to low 100s by afternoon.\n\nMost areas look fairly quiet overnight and into early Saturday\nmorning. A few showers may approach the Treasure Coast before\ndaybreak.\n\nWeekend...\n\nWe will keep our direct tap into Caribbean moisture as southerly\nflow persists through the weekend. Scattered to numerous showers and\nstorms (60-70%) are expected to redevelop in the afternoon hours as\nthe atmosphere destabilizes. We will continue to monitor locations\nthat are already saturated for potential flooding. The storms will\nmove fairly quickly from south to north, with locally gusty winds\nof 40-50 mph still expected in the strongest storms. Winds should\nturn southeasterly and become breezy on Saturday.\n\nWarm and humid weather will persist, with highs in the mid/upper\n80s and peak heat indices in the upper 90s to around 105 degrees.\n\nNext Week...\n\nA weak front is expected to settle through Central Florida\non Monday. This feature should bring modified continental\nair southward, ushering in an end to this unsettled weather \npattern. Guidance suggests that dew points may dip into the 60s as\nearly as Monday afternoon. Drier-than-normal total moisture is \nthen forecast to stay overhead through at least Wednesday, with \nslow moistening late in the work week.\n\nMostly dry conditions are forecast, with cooler morning lows in\nthe mid 60s to low 70s transitioning to warm afternoons well into\nthe 80s to near 90.",
